# Why a hard margin breaks

::default::

<MarginDemo>

<template #step0="{ nTrain }">

{{ nTrain }} training points. One orange point has landed in the middle of the blue group: an **outlier**.

</template>

<template #step1="{ mode, margin }">

<div v-if="mode === 'hard'">

A hard margin allows **no** training mistakes, so the line must tilt to squeeze past the outlier. Its margin collapses to {{ margin }}.

</div>
<div v-else>

A soft margin lets the outlier be wrong. The line stays where it makes sense, with a margin of {{ margin }}.

</div>

</template>

<template #step2="{ nTest }">

Now we overlay {{ nTest }} **test** points: new data from the same two groups, which the model never saw.

</template>

<template #default="{ mode, testErrors, nTest }">

<div v-if="mode === 'hard'">

**{{ testErrors }} of {{ nTest }}** test points are misclassified. One outlier ruined the line.

</div>
<div v-else>

**{{ testErrors }} of {{ nTest }}** test points are misclassified. Tolerating one training mistake gave a better model.

</div>

</template>

</MarginDemo>

